WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.692
Malware scanning can reduce risk in
document repositories,

2
00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:06.690
enterprise attachments, and files
exchanged between users.

3
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.495
Its usefulness depends on the threat
model,

4
00:00:09.495 --> 00:00:12.590
scanner coverage, definition updates, and
operational reliability.

5
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.071
Scanners can miss threats, fail, or time
out.

6
00:00:16.071 --> 00:00:19.553
Treat a clean result as one check in a

7
00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:23.132
broader acceptance policy, with a defined
meaning and a

8
00:00:23.132 --> 00:00:26.807
recorded result for the particular object
that was examined.

9
00:00:26.807 --> 00:00:29.999
When required checks happen
asynchronously, receive into restricted

10
00:00:29.999 --> 00:00:32.900
quarantine storage and track an explicit
state.

11
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.189
Validate the file, perform required
scanning or transformation,

12
00:00:36.189 --> 00:00:39.090
then promote only after the checks
succeed.

13
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.798
A mandatory scanner outage must leave the

14
00:00:41.798 --> 00:00:44.700
object unavailable rather than silently
approving it.

15
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.408
Tie results and promotion to an immutable

16
00:00:47.408 --> 00:00:50.309
object version or another reliable content
identity.

17
00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:53.501
Otherwise, replacing bytes after
inspection can separate the

18
00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:57.176
published object from the evidence used to
approve it.

19
00:00:57.176 --> 00:00:58.917
Authorization continues after upload.

20
00:00:58.917 --> 00:01:02.786
Replacing, viewing, downloading, deleting,
and sharing an attachment each

21
00:01:02.786 --> 00:01:06.461
require the appropriate permission for its
resource and tenant.

22
00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:08.782
Resolve ownership from trusted records and

23
00:01:08.782 --> 00:01:11.297
recheck it where the operation occurs.

24
00:01:11.297 --> 00:01:13.715
Handle retries and cleanup deliberately:
interrupted

25
00:01:13.715 --> 00:01:16.713
uploads, abandoned multipart sessions,
failed processors, and

26
00:01:16.713 --> 00:01:19.228
rejected files should not accumulate
forever.

27
00:01:19.228 --> 00:01:22.420
Cleanup must target only the failed
operation's objects,

28
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.095
and its own failures need recovery or
operational visibility.

29
00:01:26.095 --> 00:01:28.996
Test the complete lifecycle through real
adapters.

30
00:01:28.996 --> 00:01:31.898
Cover oversized requests, excessive file
counts, unsupported

31
00:01:31.898 --> 00:01:34.413
formats, extension and content mismatches,
malformed

32
00:01:34.413 --> 00:01:37.507
files, filename edge cases, and
replacement collisions.

33
00:01:37.507 --> 00:01:40.699
Include unauthorized targets and cross
tenant downloads, escaped

34
00:01:40.699 --> 00:01:44.471
archive destinations, excessive expansion
or dimensions, processor timeouts, scanner

35
00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:47.953
failure, unsafe response types, and
partial upload cleanup.

36
00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:50.274
Assert that rejected or pending objects

37
00:01:50.274 --> 00:01:52.402
are neither published nor retrievable.

38
00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:55.207
Trace the request through parsing and
limits,

39
00:01:55.207 --> 00:01:58.592
authorization, validation, storage,
processing, publication, and download.

40
00:01:58.592 --> 00:02:01.687
This review exposes gaps that a successful
upload

41
00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:04.492
response cannot reveal, especially when
background workers

42
00:02:04.492 --> 00:02:07.780
and object storage participate in the same
operation.

